cff-version: 1.2.0
message: "If you use this software, please cite it as below."
type: software
title: "Syntonic Engine: one formula, three facts about the machine, no tuning"
abstract: >-
  One formula, three facts about the machine, no tuning: the engine sets the time window of a correction or an
  estimate from statistics measured in the signal, by a law derived from first principles (Theorem U' of the
  Syntony Principle); its only machine-specific inputs are three facts measured once: the loop timing and delay,
  the actuator's authority in a correction loop or the fast sensor's drift in an estimator, and access to the
  read noise. The law sets a window, not a loop gain; stability stays with the machine.
  Implemented in Python, C++ and Rust, which agree to about 1e-14 rad. On
  synthetic benches it is at parity with hardened Bayesian Kalman banks within its class and behind them where a
  second-order model applies; on the simulated MAESTRO 1 joint bench it matches a tuned PID at the baton tip; in a
  simulated quadrotor attitude estimator, with the gyro's drift measured once at rest, it lands within a few percent
  of the best fixed window under moderate changes of the vibration (it overshoots it when the vibration rises
  x256), at parity with a tuned Kalman filter under steady vibration, and beats a fixed-noise
  Kalman filter when the vibration rises; on logs of a real quadrotor on the ground, the measured noise and drift
  matrices predict that estimating roll and pitch jointly, as an EKF does, would gain under 0.5 % (RMS) over the
  law axis by axis. The law has not yet run as an estimator on hardware. The
  hypothesis under test is portability across machines of different physics and, for the law, the living.
